    <!-- V. Chapter 4 Intro: Terminology Primer -->
    <section id="shentong-core" class="space-y-8 scroll-mt-24">
      <ChapterHeader
        chapter="Chapter 04"
        title="Doctrinal Core: Shentong vs. Rangtong"
        lede="At the heart of the Jonang tradition’s distinct identity is its philosophical formulation of emptiness (Śūnyatā), articulated through the dual framework of the Two Truths:"
      />

      <!-- Foundational Terminology Primer -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10 space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-display text-ink font-normal m-0">
          Key Terms: What Do "Self-Nature" and "Buddha-Nature" Actually Mean?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed font-serif m-0">
          Before examining the Rangtong–Shentong debate, two foundational concepts must be clearly understood. The entire disagreement turns on what these terms refer to and whether one of them points to something real:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider">Term 1</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-ink m-0">Self-Nature (Svabhāva / རང་བཞིན།)</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <em>Svabhāva</em> literally means "own-being" or "self-nature." It refers to the hypothetical quality that would make something exist <strong>independently, from its own side, without depending on causes, conditions, parts, or a perceiving mind</strong>. A table, for instance, seems solidly real — but it depends on legs, a surface, wood, a carpenter, and a concept in the observer's mind. It has no autonomous essence holding it in existence. All everyday objects, thoughts, emotions, and even the sense of a personal self are like this: they appear, but nothing inside them exists on its own power.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <strong>All Buddhist schools agree</strong> that ordinary phenomena lack <em>svabhāva</em>. This universal consensus — that tables, persons, feelings, and atoms are not self-sustaining — is the baseline meaning of "emptiness" (<em>Śūnyatā</em>).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-accent/30 space-y-3 bg-gradient-to-br from-surface-subtle to-gold/5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider">Term 2</span>
            </div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-ink m-0">Buddha-Nature (Tathāgatagarbha / བདེ་གཤེགས་སྙིང་པོ།)</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              <em>Tathāgatagarbha</em> literally means "the womb (or seed-essence) of the Thus-Gone One." It refers to <strong>whatever it is in every sentient being that makes the attainment of Buddhahood — the complete and irreversible awakening to the nature of reality, characterized by omniscient wisdom, unconditional compassion, and freedom from all suffering — possible</strong>. All Mahāyāna schools accept that something like this exists — otherwise the path would be pointless. But the schools disagree sharply about what it <em>is</em>.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0">
              The critical question is: <strong>is Buddha-nature an unhewn block of marble</strong> — raw material with workability but no finished features, requiring years of a sculptor's labor (the path of merit and wisdom) to produce a statue that did not previously exist? <strong>Or is it a golden statue already complete beneath a thick layer of dried mud</strong> — fully formed and always present, needing only the removal of what covers it? The first image captures the Rangtong reading; the second, the Shentong reading. The disagreement is not about whether Buddha-nature is "real," but about whether the qualities of Buddhahood are <em>newly produced</em> by the path or <em>revealed</em> when adventitious obscurations are cleared away.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

