                <span>🏛️ Classical Analogy:</span>
                <span class="text-ink">The Unhewn Block of Marble</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
                Buddha-nature is <strong>raw material with workable potential</strong>, but has no finished features yet. Like a block of marble, it requires years of sustained effort by a sculptor (accumulating merit and wisdom over eons) to produce a statue that did not previously exist. The enlightened qualities of a Buddha are <em>newly produced</em> by the path.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Deep Doctrinal Explanation -->
            <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink/90 font-serif leading-relaxed">
              <p class="m-0">
                The Rangtong position holds that <strong>all phenomena without exception — including ultimate truth itself — are empty of self-nature (<em>svabhāva</em>)</strong>. Emptiness is defined as a <em>non-affirming negative</em> (<em>prasajya-pratiṣedha</em>): the sheer absence of inherent existence, full stop. It does not point to any hidden positive ground behind the negation.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0">
                On this view, when the Buddha spoke of "Buddha-nature" in the Third Turning sūtras, he was using <strong>provisional, pedagogical language (<em>neyārtha</em>)</strong> designed to encourage sentient beings — not describing an actually existent luminous entity. Buddha-nature is simply the <em>mere emptiness of the mind</em>, its natural openness to transformation.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

          <!-- Bottom Denial Box -->
          <div class="pt-4 border-t border-border space-y-1.5 bg-surface-subtle/70 -mx-6 sm:-mx-8 lg:-mx-10 -mb-6 sm:-mb-8 lg:-mb-10 p-6 sm:p-8 lg:p-10 rounded-b-3xl relative z-10">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider block">
              What Rangtong Denies:
            </span>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              That ultimate reality possesses any positive, inherent qualities of its own. Everything — including the deepest truth — is characterized by the bare absence of self-nature. There is nothing "behind" or "beyond" emptiness.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Right Column: Shentong (Ultimate Truth / Other-Emptiness) -->
