          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-gold/40 space-y-1 bg-gradient-to-br from-surface-subtle to-gold/5">
            <span class="font-bold text-accent-secondary block font-mono text-xs">Shentong:</span>
            <p class="m-0 text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
              <em>"Conventional things are empty of their own nature, but ultimate reality is a radiant awareness empty only of the temporary stains that cover it — and that awareness is replete with the qualities of Buddhahood."</em>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif pt-1">
          Both traditions resolutely reject any personal ego-self (<em>ātman</em>). The Shentong term "True Self" (<em>paramātman</em>) refers strictly to the complete transcendence of conceptual dualism — the indestructible, luminous nature of mind itself.
        </p>
